## **13. My biggest recommendation**

Don't make Snake City just a collection of features.

Make it a world where the features depend on one another.

For example:

- Your movement abilities determine which traps you can bypass.
- Your engineering knowledge determines which machines you can operate.
- Your combat skills determine how you survive encounters.
- Your Soul Clan discipline determines how you approach difficult situations.
- Your evolution determines which previously inaccessible areas you can enter.
- Your puzzle-solving unlocks new routes, abilities and discoveries.

That creates an interconnected gameplay ecosystem.

For design references, these are useful starting points:

- Portal — spatial reasoning and physical puzzle interactions.
- Sekiro: Shadows Die Twice — posture, timing, parrying and combat mastery.
- Hollow Knight — movement progression and interconnected exploration.
- The Legend of Zelda: Breath of the Wild — systemic environmental interactions.

One useful engineering principle from real game-development examples is to reuse mechanics across both puzzles and combat, rather than building separate one-off interactions.&#x20;

